ft the safety of the harbor behind. The open water stretched out before him, a vast canvas of blue and white that invited him to explore its endless secrets. A gentle breeze filled his sails, carrying him further from the shore as the world below faded into a distant memory. The horizon shimmered with a heat haze, blurring the line between the deep blue sea and the endless sky above. A single seagull circled high above the mast, its cry echoing across the silent expanse as Miller settled into the rhythm of the waves, content to let the current carry him wherever it pleased. A distant thunderclap rumbled from the west, a subtle warning that the weather was shifting once more, prompting Miller to check his barometer with a renewed sense of vigilance. The needle dipped sharply, confirming his fears, and he quickly began to trim the sails, preparing to turn the boat back toward the shelter of the coast before the storm could catch him. He spun the wheel with practiced precision, angling the bow into the oncoming wind as the first heavy drops of rain began to pelt the deck. The rain intensified rapidly, turning the once-calm sea into a chaotic gray expanse that threatened to swallow his small vessel whole. Miller gripped the wheel tightly, his knuckles white, as he fought to maintain his course against the violent surge of the returning storm. A blinding flash of lightning illuminated the towering wave directly ahead, forcing him to yank the throttle to full power in a desperate bid to outrun the wall of water. The boat surged forward, the engine screaming in protest as the massive wave loomed closer, its crest beginning to curl over the bow with a deafening roar. Miller braced himself against the wheel, his eyes locked on the churning foam, knowing that the next few seconds would determine whether he would see the sun set on this day. The wave crested and broke over the bow, engulfing the deck in a torrent of icy water that Miller barely had time to brace against before